::ENG3_U02_L02_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Passage: In a mountain village, a baker saved one loaf each day for travelers who arrived after sunset. Years later, when snow blocked the pass, those same travelers returned with carts of flour. Which universal theme is best supported? {
=Kindness offered to others can return as support in a time of need.#Correct. The baker's generosity later becomes help.
~Bakers should never share food with travelers.#This contradicts the positive outcome.
~Snow always helps communities survive.#The snow creates a problem, not help.
~Travelers cannot be trusted.#The travelers return with help.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Passage: In a crowded apartment building, a teenager repaired neighbors' phones without charging them. When her own computer broke before an interview, the neighbors found parts, tools, and a quiet room where she could prepare. Which theme matches the passage? {
~Technology prevents people from cooperating.#Technology is part of the setting, but cooperation is positive.
=Generosity can build a community of support.#Correct. The teenager's help leads to the neighbors helping her.
~Interviews are impossible without neighbors.#This is too narrow and literal.
~People should avoid repairing phones.#The passage presents repair positively.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: A baker shares loaves with travelers. Later, travelers bring flour during a snowstorm. Text B: A teenager repairs phones for neighbors. Later, neighbors help fix her computer. What shared theme do the texts develop? {
~Different jobs require different tools.#This is true on the surface but not the shared theme.
~Food is more useful than technology.#This compares details but misses the theme.
=Acts of service can create reciprocal care.#Correct. Both texts show service returning as care.
~Snowstorms and interviews are identical problems.#The problems differ.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] How do Text A and Text B develop the shared theme differently? Text A uses a mountain village and travelers; Text B uses an apartment building and technology repair. {
~Text A has no setting, while Text B has a setting.#Both texts have settings.
~Text A rejects generosity, while Text B supports it.#Both support generosity.
~Text A and Text B have identical plots.#The plots are similar in theme but not identical.
=Text A develops the theme through survival supplies, while Text B develops it through practical help and technology.#Correct. This explains different development across settings.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which is a complete universal theme statement? {
~Memory#This is only a topic.
~A character remembers the past#This is a plot idea, not a theme.
=Remembering the past can help people choose a wiser future.#Correct. This is a complete insight about life.
~The story has memory in it#This is vague and incomplete.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: An old sailor teaches children to read stars after ships begin using machines. Text B: A coding mentor teaches students to sketch ideas before opening laptops. What theme do both texts share? {
~New tools make old skills worthless.#Both texts argue the opposite.
=Foundational skills remain valuable even when tools change.#Correct. Both texts show older or basic skills still mattering.
~People should reject all technology.#Neither text rejects technology completely.
~Stars and laptops have the same purpose.#This is a surface-level false comparison.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which evidence from Text A supports the theme that foundational skills remain valuable? Text A: An old sailor teaches children to read stars after ships begin using machines. "Tools change," he said, "but people still need to know how to look up." {
~"old sailor"#This identifies a character but not the theme directly.
~"ships began using machines"#This gives context but not the full theme.
="people still need to know how to look up"#Correct. This directly supports the value of foundational skill.
~"children"#This identifies learners but does not state the theme.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which evidence from Text B supports the theme that thinking matters before using tools? Text B: A coding mentor taught students to sketch their ideas on paper before opening a laptop. "Screens are useful," she said, "but thinking begins before the screen turns on." {
~"coding mentor"#This identifies the speaker but not the theme.
="thinking begins before the screen turns on"#Correct. This directly supports the theme about thinking before tools.
~"students"#This identifies people but not the theme.
~"laptop"#This names the tool, but the quote explains the theme.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A is set at sea, and Text B is set in a modern classroom. Why can they still share a universal theme? {
~Universal themes depend on identical settings.#This is false.
~Universal themes only happen in modern stories.#This is false.
=Universal themes can appear in different times and places because they address human concerns.#Correct. Setting can change while the human concern remains.
~Universal themes do not need evidence.#Theme analysis requires evidence.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Passage: A queen in an old tale gives away her crown to save a village. A mayor in a modern story gives up his office to expose the truth. Which theme do both plots support? {
~Leadership means keeping power at all costs.#Both characters give up power.
~Crowns and offices are the same object.#This is a surface comparison.
~Villages and modern towns cannot share themes.#They can share human concerns.
=True leadership may require sacrifice.#Correct. Both characters give up status for others or truth.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which statement best tracks a theme across two texts? {
=Both texts explore courage, but the first shows physical courage while the second shows moral courage.#Correct. This names a shared theme and explains different development.
~Both texts have characters.#This is too general.
~The first text is old and the second text is new.#This names a difference but not a theme.
~Both texts are exactly the same.#Theme comparison does not require identical texts.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: A farmer plants seeds during a drought. Text B: A student starts a club after everyone says no one will join. Which universal theme best connects the texts? {
~Farming is the same as school.#This is a surface comparison.
=Hope often begins before success is visible.#Correct. Both characters act before results are certain.
~Droughts always improve schools.#This is unsupported.
~Clubs are more important than crops.#This is not the shared theme.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which is NOT a strong universal theme statement? {
~Responsibility can grow when one person acts first.#This is a complete theme.
~Fear can be faced through small acts of courage.#This is a complete theme.
=Friendship#Correct. Friendship is a topic, not a complete theme statement.
~Memory can guide people through change.#This is a complete theme.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: A child in a folktale returns a lost coin. Text B: A teenager in a city returns a lost wallet. Which shared theme is best? {
=Honesty matters even when no one is watching.#Correct. Both actions show honesty across different settings.
~Cities are more honest than folktales.#This is unsupported.
~Lost objects always return by magic.#This is unsupported.
~Coins and wallets are identical objects.#They are related but not identical.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] What is the main difference between a topic and a theme? {
=A topic names the subject, while a theme states an insight about that subject.#Correct. This explains the difference clearly.
~A topic requires evidence, but a theme does not.#Both can be discussed with evidence, and theme must be supported.
~A topic is always longer than a theme.#Length does not define the difference.
~A theme is only one word.#A theme should usually be a complete idea.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: A village shares tools to repair a roof before winter. Text B: Neighbors share internet access so students can finish assignments. Which theme do both develop? {
=Communities become stronger when people share resources.#Correct. Both texts show shared resources solving problems.
~Winter is the same as school.#This is a surface comparison.
~Internet access is more important than shelter.#This is not the shared theme.
~Tools are only physical objects.#Text B's support is not only physical tools.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which response best compares theme development? {
~Text A and Text B both use words.#This is not meaningful theme analysis.
~Text A is about a storm, and Text B is about a failure, so they cannot share a theme.#Different events can share a theme.
~Text A is better because it has weather.#This is unsupported and not analytical.
=Text A develops resilience through survival after a storm, while Text B develops resilience through rebuilding after a public failure.#Correct. This compares how the same theme develops differently.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: A young messenger keeps walking despite a mountain path. Text B: A nurse keeps working despite a crowded hospital. Which theme best connects them? {
~Mountains and hospitals are the same setting.#They are different settings.
=Perseverance means continuing when circumstances are difficult.#Correct. Both characters continue through difficulty.
~Messages are always medical.#This is unsupported.
~Crowded places prevent all courage.#This is too absolute and unsupported.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which evidence best supports a theme about belonging? Passage: The new student sat alone until the lunch table shifted, one chair at a time, leaving a space in the middle. {
~"new student"#This identifies a character but does not show belonging by itself.
~"sat alone"#This shows isolation, but not the movement toward belonging.
="leaving a space in the middle"#Correct. This detail shows others making room for the student.
~"lunch table"#This gives setting, not theme by itself.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which pair of texts is most likely to share a universal theme? {
~One text lists train schedules, and one text gives a recipe.#These are not literary theme-focused texts.
~One text defines a word, and one text labels a diagram.#These are unlikely to develop literary themes.
~One text gives weather data, and one text gives a phone number.#These do not develop literary themes.
=One text shows a soldier forgiving an enemy, and another shows a student forgiving a friend.#Correct. Both can develop a theme about forgiveness across different settings.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: A character leaves home to protect family honor. Text B: A character speaks the truth even when it disappoints friends. Which shared theme is strongest? {
=Integrity may require difficult personal choices.#Correct. Both characters make hard choices based on values.
~Travel is always honorable.#Only Text A includes leaving home.
~Friendship always prevents truth.#Text B shows truth despite disappointment.
~Families and friends are identical.#They are different relationships.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which sentence best uses academic vocabulary for theme analysis? {
~The texts are about stuff people do.#This is too vague.
~Theme is when there are characters.#This misunderstands theme.
~The thing in both stories is good.#This is vague and informal.
=Both texts develop the universal theme that responsibility grows through action.#Correct. This uses academic vocabulary and states a complete theme.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: An apprentice admits a mistake to a master craftsperson. Text B: A lab assistant reports an error before the experiment continues. Which theme do both texts develop? {
~Mistakes should always be hidden.#Both characters admit errors.
~Crafts and science are identical.#They are different fields.
=Honesty can protect the quality of shared work.#Correct. Both texts show honesty preventing greater harm.
~Apprentices and lab assistants never learn.#This is unsupported.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which statement best avoids overgeneralizing a theme? {
~All people always help others when they can.#This is too absolute.
~No one can ever change without suffering.#This is too absolute.
~Every old story teaches the same lesson.#This is too broad.
=Some texts suggest that help offered freely can strengthen a community.#Correct. This states a theme without claiming it applies to every person or every text.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: A character lights a lamp for travelers in an old village. Text B: A character posts clear directions online during an emergency. Which comparison best tracks the shared theme? {
~Both texts prove lamps and websites are the same.#This is a surface comparison.
~Only old villages can show responsibility.#Text B also shows responsibility.
=Both texts show responsibility through guidance, but one uses a physical light and the other uses digital directions.#Correct. This tracks the shared theme and different development.
~Neither text includes a helpful action.#Both include helpful guidance.
}
